    "result_text": "I've implemented `answer()` in `app/chat.py`, and all 6 tests pass, including 4 new ones.\n\n**How it works**\n- Each attempt calls `iter_answer()` again and joins the text only once the stream finishes. A dropped attempt's partial text is thrown away, so the reply never contains pieces of a failed try.\n- **Network failure or drop** (`openai.APIConnectionError`): it logs a WARNING on `app.chat` and starts over, up to `ATTEMPTS` (3) tries in total. If all of them fail, it raises `UpstreamUnavailable`, linked to the last error.\n- **Timeout** (`openai.APITimeoutError`): it raises `UpstreamTimeout` straight away, with no retry.\n- The client is used exactly as given. I added no dependencies.\n\n**Points worth knowing**\n- **The error message in the log.** The pinned openai 3.19.2 turns every network error into `APIConnectionError(\"Connection error.\")`, whether it happens on connect or mid-stream. That text can't tell a connection reset from a cut-off response. The SDK keeps the real network error attached to the exception, so the warning logs that instead, e.g. `Attempt 2/3 to reach the model provider failed: RemoteProtocolError: peer closed connection without sending complete message body`.\n- **Timeouts are checked first.** In the SDK, `APITimeoutError` is a subclass of `APIConnectionError`. If the order were reversed, timeouts would be retried, which your fourth rule forbids.\n- **The client's own retries still apply.** The SDK's `max_retries` still retries failed connections inside each attempt, so the worst case is more than 3 real requests.\n- **`history` is copied into a list first.** Otherwise a one-shot iterable, like a generator, would be empty on the second attempt.\n\n**Tests** (`tests/test_chat.py`): the fake errors are the SDK's own exception classes, attached to real network errors the same way the SDK does it. The tests cover:\n- a normal reply\n- a refused connection, then a mid-stream drop, then success: only the final text comes back, and both warnings carry the un"
